        </details>
        <details open class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">What is the difference between a doula and a midwife?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">A midwife is a medical professional who provides prenatal care, delivers your baby, and manages clinical aspects of birth. A doula is a non-medical support person who stays with you continuously through labor, focusing on comfort, emotional support, and advocacy. A midwife handles the medical side. A doula handles the emotional and physical side. Many families hire both.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">How much does a doula cost?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">In most US cities, birth doulas charge between $800 and $2,500 for a full package including prenatal visits, on-call availability, continuous labor support, and postpartum visits. The national average is around $1,200 to $1,500. Some doulas offer sliding-scale fees, and Medicaid covers doulas in more than a dozen states.</div>
